From 82bd7060bbc0b2a40bf03886adffb56221154109 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 19:10:20 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(doctor): argv masking fails closed - show identifiers,
 mask the rest

Review of the per-shape rules found them leaking 9-32% of 16-19 character
random tokens while masking model ids, camelCase names and Hermes ids, and
a nested-value recursion crash. Patching shape by shape could not get both
right, so the mask is inverted: an argument is shown only when it parses as
an identifier, and masked otherwise.

- Identifier grammar: pieces between separators must be numbers, short
  alphanumerics, words (lower/UPPER/Title/camelCase) or letter runs that
  read as words (common letter pairs from /usr/share/dict/words plus real
  identifiers, vowels, rare letters, consonant clusters); each word chunk is
  also judged with its symbols removed, so symbols cannot cut a secret into
  innocent pieces; many letter/digit alternations or 16+ hex mean generated.
  UUIDs, Hermes/cloud ids (t_, proc_, i-...), short hex (commits), paths
  that exist on this host, and comma/pipe lists item by item are kept.
- Names still mark values secret whatever they look like: secret-named
  flags (fail closed), NAME=value, Header: value, user:pass@host (not
  package or image specs), programs' own password flags (mysql, sshpass,
  redis-cli, openssl, mongo, ldap*, sqlcmd, keytool, 7z, zip, ssh-keygen,
  curl/smbclient/lftp user:pass in every spelling), also behind sudo,
  docker exec, env and bash -c; command wrappers (sshpass) hand over to the
  wrapped program, so ssh -p 2222 stays visible.
- Nesting is depth-capped (no RecursionError).

Measured: at most ~3% of 16-character generated secrets of any common shape
are shown (under 1% from 32), 0.2% of this repo's identifiers are masked,
and on example hosts only Chromium's base64 --gpu-preferences
blobs are masked among live process arguments. Tests: rate-based fuzz per
shape and length, identifier corpus, named/program secrets, wrappers,
Hermes arguments, deep nesting.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
